    "q-70680": {
      "dom_id": "step-q70680",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "If $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=4$ for some $x$, then find the value of $\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x+2\\pi )-3.$",
      "worked_solution": "The period of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ is $\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x+n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=2$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x+(2)\\cdot \\pi )=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x+2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=4,$ then $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x+2\\pi )=4.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x+2\\pi )-3 & =\\frac{1}{2}(4)-3 \\\\  & =2-3 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
