      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "If $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{3}{10},$ then $5\\operatorname{cot}⁡(-x+9\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14264109/assets/6eb672fe024f024f20c7c3c80947d1b47bade8f998e705d6f411ae3f298ecde2.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ is $\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡(-x)=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(-x+9\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(-x+\\pi \\cdot 9) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(-x) & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cot}⁡(-x+9\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2} & =5\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{10})-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{10}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
